+The corresponding objective is $F(\mu)=R(f_\mu)+\int_\Omega V\,d\mu$, whenever the scalar integral is finite, with $R:\mathcal H\to[0,\infty)$ a risk functional and $V:\Omega\to[0,\infty)$ a regularizer. For the empirical particle law with integer $m\ge1$ defined on measurable $A\subseteq\Omega$ by $\mu_m(A)=m^{-1}\#\{j:(m a_j,w_j,b_j)\in A\}$, one has $f_{\mu_m}(x)=\sum_{j=1}^m a_j\sigma(w_j\cdot x+b_j)$, so every positive-width finite one-hidden-layer network is a particle-measure predictor (with an optional constant output bias added separately). For width $m=0$, the zero finite nonnegative measure $\mu=0$ has Bochner predictor $f_0=0$ and represents the empty-sum network before adding its optional output bias. This zero measure is a finite-predictor representation; it is not a probability law for the Wasserstein-flow clause. A feature-energy Wasserstein gradient flow means an $AC^2_{\rm loc}$ curve of probability measures satisfying the finite-action continuity equation with Borel velocity equal almost everywhere to the negative minimum-norm element of the constrained subdifferential of the first-variation potential. This is the earlier model-flow definition; no existence or uniqueness follows from this definition. The flow use below requires all of the feature-energy regularity hypotheses. In particular, the classical ReLU feature map is not asserted here to meet those hypotheses merely because it is positively homogeneous.
 
